IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cor-dates`
IMPORT `cor-core`
IMPORT `cor-seat`

-- Coroners Act 2003 (SA) -- cases for the family member's seat (cor-seat.l4), with some on cor-core.l4 functions the
-- seat composes (State death, death in custody, the s 3 kinds of reportable death). Every expected value was worked by
-- hand from the deposited text (the section is named on each group), never copied from the encoding's output. Edges:
-- the last day of the s 27(3) month and the day after, a findings day at the end of a month (31 January, 28 February,
-- a leap year), the year end; the s 37A(2) 20th anniversary and the day after (and 29 February in a century year); the
-- s 25(5) 6 months over a short February; each limb of every "or" and each conjunct of every "and"; and the order in
-- which provisions displace one another.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases: the family member`

-- A shorthand for the 16 fields of the core's death facts, in their order.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN, d IS A BOOLEAN, e IS A BOOLEAN, f IS A BOOLEAN, g IS A BOOLEAN, h IS A BOOLEAN, i IS A BOOLEAN, j IS A BOOLEAN, k IS A BOOLEAN, l IS A BOOLEAN, m IS A BOOLEAN, n IS A BOOLEAN, o IS A BOOLEAN, p IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Death facts`
`facts` a b c d e f g h i j k l m n o p MEANS
    `Death facts` WITH
        `state death` IS a
        `unexpected, unnatural, unusual, violent or unknown cause` IS b
        `on an aircraft in flight or a vessel on a voyage` IS c
        `in custody` IS d
        `during, as a result of, or within 24 hours of a surgical, invasive medical or diagnostic procedure or its anaesthetic` IS e
        `the procedure is one the regulations exclude` IS f
        `died outside a hospital` IS g
        `within 24 hours of discharge after being an inpatient` IS h
        `within 24 hours of seeking emergency treatment at a hospital` IS i
        `a protected or supervised person at death` IS j
        `during, as a result of, or within 24 hours of treatment consented to under the guardianship or consent laws` IS k
        `no cause of death certificate given to the Registrar` IS l
        `circumstances prescribed by the regulations` IS m
        `died outside SA` IS n
        `a corresponding authorisation was issued elsewhere` IS o
        `voluntary assisted dying substance under the 2021 Act` IS p

`yes reportable` MEANS `yes: it is a reportable death (s 3(1))`
`no kind` MEANS `no: none of the kinds of reportable death in s 3(1)(a)-(i) applies`
`not State` MEANS `no: it is not a State death, so it cannot be a reportable death (s 3(1))`
`VAD` MEANS `no: the death resulted from a voluntary assisted dying substance administered under the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2021 (s 3(2a))`
`interstate` MEANS `no: the person died outside South Australia and a coroner elsewhere has issued a corresponding authorisation for the body (s 3(2)); an inquest may still be held if the State Coroner considers it necessary or desirable (s 21(1)(b)(i))`

`free` MEANS `not detained, held or being apprehended`
`detained` MEANS `detained in a place in South Australia under an Act or law (home detention included, or absent in the custody of an escort)`
`no order` MEANS `not under a mental health inpatient order or a guardianship detention order`
`MH ward wholly` MEANS `died of certified natural causes under an inpatient treatment order, in a ward wholly set aside for mental illness`
`MH ward not wholly` MEANS `died of certified natural causes under an inpatient treatment order, in a ward not wholly set aside for mental illness`
`GAA order` MEANS `died of certified natural causes while subject to an order under s 32(1)(b) of the Guardianship and Administration Act 1993`

§§ `s 3(1) State death: each limb of the "or"`

#ASSERT `is a State death (s 3(1))` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `is a State death (s 3(1))` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `is a State death (s 3(1))` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `is a State death (s 3(1))` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `is a State death (s 3(1))` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `is a State death (s 3(1))` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a State death (s 3(1))` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE))

§§ `s 3(1), s 21(4), (5): death in custody`

-- (a) to (d), and the two situations outside them: absent without escort ("but not otherwise"); held outside the State
-- under another jurisdiction's law only ((b)(ii) is "at any place within the State").
#ASSERT `Was it a death in custody` `detained` `no order`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in custody` `being apprehended or held, anywhere, by a person authorised under a South Australian law` `no order`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in custody` `being apprehended or held in South Australia by a person authorised under another jurisdiction's law` `no order`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in custody` `evading apprehension by a person authorised to apprehend or hold` `no order`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in custody` `escaping or attempting to escape from detention or from a person holding or apprehending` `no order`
#ASSERT (NOT (`Was it a death in custody` `absent from the place of detention, not in the custody of an escort` `no order`))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Was it a death in custody` `being apprehended or held outside South Australia by a person authorised only under another jurisdiction's law` `no order`))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Was it a death in custody` `free` `no order`))
-- s 21(4): a certified natural-causes death under an inpatient order in a ward wholly set aside is taken to be one.
#ASSERT `Was it a death in custody` `free` `MH ward wholly`
-- s 21(5) speaks only for s 21(1)(a), so the s 3(1) answer is unchanged by it.
#ASSERT (NOT (`Was it a death in custody` `free` `MH ward not wholly`))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Was it a death in custody` `free` `GAA order`))
#ASSERT `Was it a death in custody` `detained` `GAA order`
-- For s 21(1)(a), s 21(5) prevails even over the custody limbs (fork F5).
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a death in custody for s 21(1)(a)` `detained` `GAA order`))
#ASSERT `is a death in custody for s 21(1)(a)` `detained` `no order`
#ASSERT `is a death in custody for s 21(1)(a)` `free` `MH ward wholly`

§§ `s 3(1), (2), (2a): reportable death`

-- Each kind (a) to (i) alone, on a State death.
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
-- (d) with a procedure the regulations exclude: not that kind.
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `no kind`
-- (e) needs a place other than a hospital AND one of the 24-hour events.
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `no kind`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `no kind`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
-- None of the kinds: an expected natural death with a certificate.
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `no kind`
-- Not a State death: no kind can save it.
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `not State`
-- s 3(2a): voluntary assisted dying, even with a kind (unnatural) present.
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `VAD`
-- s 3(2): both conjuncts needed.
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS `interstate`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `is it a reportable death (s 3)` (`facts` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS `yes reportable`
-- s 21(1)(b)(i) "would, but for section 3(2), have been": the interstate case counts, the VAD case does not.
#ASSERT `is reportable or would be but for s 3(2)` (`facts` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT (NOT (`is reportable or would be but for s 3(2)` (`facts` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE)))

-- The seat question, end to end: a road death in Adelaide (unnatural cause); and a resident who died in a Victorian
-- hospital after a fall there, released by the Victorian coroner (State death by residence, s 3(2) applies).
#ASSERT `Is my family member's death a reportable death` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE `free` `no order` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes reportable`
#ASSERT `Is my family member's death a reportable death` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE `free` `no order` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `interstate`
-- A prisoner dying of a certified natural cause in a South Australian prison: kind (c) through the custody situation.
#ASSERT `Is my family member's death a reportable death` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `detained` `no order` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes reportable`
-- The s 21(4) deeming reaches kind (c) too.
#ASSERT `Is my family member's death a reportable death` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `free` `MH ward wholly` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes reportable`
-- An expected death at home, certified: none of the kinds.
#ASSERT `Is my family member's death a reportable death` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `free` `no order` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no kind`

§§ `s 21(1): must there be an inquest`

`custody inquest` MEANS `yes: the Coroner's Court must hold an inquest, because it is a death in custody (s 21(1)(a))`
`chosen inquest` MEANS `yes: the Coroner's Court must hold an inquest, because the State Coroner considers it necessary or desirable or the Attorney-General has directed one (s 21(1)(b))`
`other Act inquest` MEANS `yes: the Coroner's Court must hold an inquest, because another Act requires one (s 21(1)(c))`
`only if chosen` MEANS `only if the State Coroner considers it necessary or desirable, or the Attorney-General directs one (s 21(1)(b)); neither has happened`
`no inquest` MEANS `no: s 21(1) does not reach this event, so the Coroner's Court holds no inquest into it (ss 13(1), 21(1))`

-- (a) a death in custody: mandatory, whatever the State Coroner thinks.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a death` `detained` `no order` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `custody inquest`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a death` `free` `MH ward wholly` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `custody inquest`
-- s 21(5): the GAA and "not wholly" ward cases fall to (b)(v), discretionary.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a death` `free` `GAA order` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `only if chosen`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a death` `free` `MH ward not wholly` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `chosen inquest`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a death` `detained` `GAA order` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `only if chosen`
-- (b)(i) another reportable death.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a death` `free` `no order` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `only if chosen`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a death` `free` `no order` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `chosen inquest`
-- A death neither reportable nor (b)(v): no inquest, even if the State Coroner wants one.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a death` `free` `no order` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no inquest`
-- (b)(ii), (iii), (iv).
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `the disappearance, from any place, of a person ordinarily resident in South Australia` `free` `no order` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `only if chosen`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `the disappearance of any person from, or within, South Australia` `free` `no order` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `chosen inquest`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `a fire or accident that causes injury to a person or property` `free` `no order` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `chosen inquest`
-- (c).
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `an event another Act requires an inquest into` `free` `no order` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `other Act inquest`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest` `another event` `free` `no order` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no inquest`
#ASSERT `an inquest is required` `custody inquest`
#ASSERT (NOT (`an inquest is required` `only if chosen`))

§§ `s 21(2): a criminal charge comes first`

#ASSERT `Does a criminal charge hold up the inquest` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no bar: no one has been charged in criminal proceedings with causing the event (s 21(2))`
#ASSERT `Does a criminal charge hold up the inquest` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the inquest cannot begin or go further yet: someone has been charged with causing the event, and the criminal proceedings have not been disposed of, withdrawn or permanently stayed (s 21(2))`
#ASSERT `Does a criminal charge hold up the inquest` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the bar has lifted: the criminal proceedings have been disposed of, withdrawn or permanently stayed (s 21(2))`

§§ `ss 20A(5), 29(3), 37A(9): senior next of kin and family`

`spouse` MEANS `spouse or domestic partner (the most recent, if there was more than one)`
-- (a) the most recent spouse or partner, always.
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `spouse` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- an earlier spouse is never on the list.
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I the senior next of kin` `an earlier spouse or domestic partner` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE))
-- (b) an adult child, only if no available spouse.
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `adult child` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I the senior next of kin` `adult child` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE))
-- (c) a parent, only if no available spouse and no available adult child.
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `parent (or, if the person was a child, guardian)` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I the senior next of kin` `parent (or, if the person was a child, guardian)` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I the senior next of kin` `parent (or, if the person was a child, guardian)` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE))
-- (d) an adult sibling.
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `adult brother or sister` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I the senior next of kin` `adult brother or sister` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE))
-- (e) executor or legal personal representative, only when (a)-(d) are all missing.
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `named as an executor in the person's will` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `the person's legal personal representative immediately before the death` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I the senior next of kin` `named as an executor in the person's will` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I the senior next of kin` `another relative or person` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE))
-- Family: senior next of kin; or kin under Aboriginal kinship rules of an Aboriginal person (both parts of the definition).
#ASSERT `Am I family` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Am I family` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I family` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I family` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Am I family` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE))

§§ `ss 19, 20, 20A: attending and appearing`

#ASSERT `Can I attend the inquest` FALSE EQUALS `yes: inquests must be open to the public (s 19(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I attend the inquest` TRUE EQUALS `not while the order stands: the Court may clear the court or suppress evidence under Part 8 of the Evidence Act 1929, or in the interests of national security (s 19)`
#ASSERT `Can I appear at the inquest` TRUE EQUALS `yes: you are entitled to appear personally or by counsel and to examine and cross-examine any witness, because the Court is of the opinion that you have a sufficient interest (s 20(1)(b), (2))`
#ASSERT `Can I appear at the inquest` FALSE EQUALS `not as of right: you are entitled to appear only if the Court is of the opinion that you have a sufficient interest in the subject or result of the proceedings (s 20(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can our family's lawyer appear` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: as the family's nominated representative the lawyer is entitled to appear and to examine and cross-examine any witness; the family may apply for legal assistance with the reasonable legal costs under the Legal Services Commission Act 1977 (s 20A(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can our family's lawyer appear` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 20A does not apply: the proceedings do not relate to a death or disappearance; the lawyer may appear for you only if the Court is of the opinion that you have a sufficient interest (ss 20(1)(b), 20A(1))`
#ASSERT `Can our family's lawyer appear` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not under s 20A: the nominated representative must be a legal practitioner engaged by or for the family for these proceedings (s 20A(4)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can our family's lawyer appear` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not under s 20A yet: the family must nominate the lawyer as its nominated representative in accordance with the Court's rules (s 20A(4)(b))`

§§ `ss 23(5), 23A: answering, certificates, later use`

#ASSERT `Must I answer this question at the inquest` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: you are not required to answer the question or produce the document, because it would breach legal professional privilege (s 23(5))`
#ASSERT `Must I answer this question at the inquest` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you must answer if required; refusing or failing to answer a relevant question truthfully is a contempt of the Court (s 23(1)(e), (4)(c))`
#ASSERT `Must I answer this question at the inquest` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Court has found no reasonable grounds for your objection, so you must answer; if the Court later finds there were reasonable grounds, you must be given a certificate (s 23A(2), (6))`
#ASSERT `Must I answer this question at the inquest` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the Court cannot require you to answer: the answer may tend to incriminate you, or expose you to a penalty, under a foreign law; if you answer willingly you must be given a certificate (s 23A(3), (4)(a), (5)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must I answer this question at the inquest` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `you may be required to answer: the Court is satisfied that the interests of justice require it; if it requires you, you must answer and be given a certificate (s 23A(4), (5)(b))`
#ASSERT `Must I answer this question at the inquest` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no, unless the Court requires you to: the Court has found reasonable grounds for your objection; if you answer willingly you must be given a certificate (s 23A(3), (5)(a))`

#ASSERT `Will I get a certificate` `I objected, the Court found reasonable grounds, and I answered willingly without being required` EQUALS `yes: the Court must cause you to be given a certificate, because you answered willingly after it found reasonable grounds for your objection (s 23A(5)(a))`
#ASSERT `Will I get a certificate` `I objected, the Court found reasonable grounds, and I answered after the Court required me to` EQUALS `yes: the Court must cause you to be given a certificate, because you answered after it required you to (s 23A(4), (5)(b))`
#ASSERT `Will I get a certificate` `I objected, the Court overruled the objection, I answered, and the Court afterwards found there were reasonable grounds` EQUALS `yes: the Court must cause you to be given a certificate, because it overruled your objection and afterwards found there were reasonable grounds for it (s 23A(6))`
#ASSERT `Will I get a certificate` `I objected, the Court overruled the objection, I answered, and the Court did not find reasonable grounds` EQUALS `no: s 23A gives a certificate only where the Court finds reasonable grounds for an objection (s 23A(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `Will I get a certificate` `I answered without objecting` EQUALS `no: s 23A applies only where you object to answering on the ground of self-incrimination or a penalty (s 23A(1))`

#ASSERT `Can my inquest answer be used against me` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the answer, and anything obtained directly or indirectly because you gave it, cannot be used against you in any proceeding in a court or before a body authorised to hear evidence, even if the certificate is challenged or quashed (s 23A(8), (10))`
#ASSERT `Can my inquest answer be used against me` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes, it can: the protection does not apply to a criminal proceeding about the falsity of the answer (s 23A(9))`
#ASSERT `Can my inquest answer be used against me` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 23A gives no protection: you were not given a certificate (s 23A(8))`

§§ `s 25: what the inquest can find, copies, the Minister's 6 months`

#ASSERT `Can the inquest find this` `the cause and circumstances of the event` EQUALS `yes: the Court must give written findings, as soon as practicable after the inquest, setting out as far as ascertained the cause and circumstances of the event (s 25(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the inquest find this` `a recommendation that might prevent a similar event, or on a related matter such as care, public health or safety, or the administration of justice` EQUALS `it may: the Court may add any recommendation that in its opinion might prevent or reduce the likelihood of a similar event, or relates to a matter arising from the inquest and is appropriate (s 25(2))`
#ASSERT `Can the inquest find this` `a finding or suggestion that someone is civilly liable (must pay damages or compensation)` EQUALS `no: the Court must not make any finding, or suggestion, of criminal or civil liability (s 25(3)); liability is for a court hearing a claim or a prosecution`
#ASSERT `Can the inquest find this` `a finding or suggestion that someone is criminally liable` EQUALS `no: the Court must not make any finding, or suggestion, of criminal or civil liability (s 25(3)); liability is for a court hearing a claim or a prosecution`

`not sent` MEANS `not sent to you under s 25(4), which sends them to the Attorney-General and relevant Ministers, and to others only after an inquest into a death in custody; you may apply to the State Coroner for a transcript of the findings and recommendations (s 37(1)(d))`
#ASSERT `Will I be sent the findings` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Court must forward you a copy of its findings and recommendations as soon as practicable, because you appeared personally or by counsel at an inquest into a death in custody (s 25(4)(b)(ii))`
#ASSERT `Will I be sent the findings` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Court must forward you a copy of its findings and recommendations as soon as practicable, because in its opinion you have a sufficient interest in the matter (s 25(4)(b)(iii))`
#ASSERT `Will I be sent the findings` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not sent`
-- Not a death in custody: even a family member who appeared is not sent a copy under s 25(4).
#ASSERT `Will I be sent the findings` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not sent`

-- s 25(5): 6 months after receipt. 10 January 2025 -> 10 July 2025; 31 August 2025 -> 28 February 2026 (no 31st);
-- 15 September 2025 -> 15 March 2026 (over the year end).
#ASSERT `When do the Minister's 6 months end` (`cor date` 10 1 2025) EQUALS `cor date` 10 7 2025
#ASSERT `When do the Minister's 6 months end` (`cor date` 31 8 2025) EQUALS `cor date` 28 2 2026
#ASSERT `When do the Minister's 6 months end` (`cor date` 15 9 2025) EQUALS `cor date` 15 3 2026

§§ `s 26: re-opening`

#ASSERT `Can the inquest be re-opened` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Court must re-open the inquest, because the Attorney-General has directed the State Coroner to (s 26(1)); it may then confirm, set aside or substitute findings (s 26(2))`
#ASSERT `Can the inquest be re-opened` FALSE EQUALS `it may: the Court may re-open the inquest at any time (s 26(1)), but nothing obliges it to unless the Attorney-General directs; it may then confirm, set aside or substitute findings (s 26(2))`

§§ `s 27: appeals`

#ASSERT `Can I appeal against the finding` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may appeal to the Supreme Court, because the finding affects or may affect your pecuniary interests (s 27(1), (7)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal against the finding` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may appeal to the Supreme Court, because the finding reflects adversely on your competence in your trade, profession or occupation (s 27(1), (7)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal against the finding` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: you may appeal to the Supreme Court, because the Supreme Court is of the opinion that you have some other sufficient interest (s 27(1), (7)(c))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal against the finding` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: none of the grounds of sufficient interest in s 27(7) applies, so s 27 gives you no appeal`

-- One month after: 15 March 2025 -> 15 April 2025; 31 January 2025 -> 28 February 2025; 31 January 2024 -> 29 February
-- 2024; 28 February 2025 -> 28 March 2025 (corresponding date, fork F1); 15 December 2025 -> 15 January 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I appeal` (`cor date` 15 3 2025) EQUALS `cor date` 15 4 2025
#ASSERT `By when must I appeal` (`cor date` 31 1 2025) EQUALS `cor date` 28 2 2025
#ASSERT `By when must I appeal` (`cor date` 31 1 2024) EQUALS `cor date` 29 2 2024
#ASSERT `By when must I appeal` (`cor date` 28 2 2025) EQUALS `cor date` 28 3 2025
#ASSERT `By when must I appeal` (`cor date` 15 12 2025) EQUALS `cor date` 15 1 2026

`in time` MEANS `in time: you applied within one month after the findings were given (s 27(3))`
`late` MEANS `out of time: more than one month has passed since the findings were given; only the Supreme Court can extend the time, in its discretion, even after it has ended (s 27(3), (4))`
`extended` MEANS `out of time, but the Supreme Court has extended the time (s 27(4))`
-- The last day, and the day after.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 15 3 2025) (`cor date` 15 4 2025) FALSE EQUALS `in time`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 15 3 2025) (`cor date` 16 4 2025) FALSE EQUALS `late`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 15 3 2025) (`cor date` 16 4 2025) TRUE EQUALS `extended`
-- An extension is not needed when in time.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 15 3 2025) (`cor date` 1 4 2025) TRUE EQUALS `in time`
-- The same day as the findings.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 15 3 2025) (`cor date` 15 3 2025) FALSE EQUALS `in time`
-- The end of February.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 31 1 2025) (`cor date` 28 2 2025) FALSE EQUALS `in time`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 31 1 2025) (`cor date` 1 3 2025) FALSE EQUALS `late`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 31 1 2024) (`cor date` 29 2 2024) FALSE EQUALS `in time`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 15 12 2025) (`cor date` 15 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `in time`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 15 12 2025) (`cor date` 16 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `late`
-- Before the findings.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (`cor date` 15 3 2025) (`cor date` 14 3 2025) FALSE EQUALS `too early: there is no finding to appeal against until the findings are given (s 27(1), (3))`
-- The regulative rule itself: 31 days allowed (15 March to 15 April), day 31 in time, day 32 not.
#ASSERT `s 27(3) -- an application on this day is in time` 31 31
#ASSERT (NOT (`s 27(3) -- an application on this day is in time` 31 32))

§§ `s 28: reporting`

#ASSERT `Must I report this death` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you must immediately notify the State Coroner or a police officer of the death, and give them any information you have about it (s 28(1), (2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must I report this death` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you must immediately notify the State Coroner (for a death in custody a police officer will not do), and give any information you have about it (s 28(1), (2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must I report this death` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: you believe on reasonable grounds that the death has already been reported, or that the State Coroner is otherwise aware of it (s 28(1))`
#ASSERT `Must I report this death` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 28 applies only to a death that is or may be a reportable death`
#ASSERT `Must I give my opinion on the cause` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Must I give my opinion on the cause` TRUE TRUE FALSE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Must I give my opinion on the cause` TRUE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Must I give my opinion on the cause` FALSE TRUE TRUE))

§§ `Penalties`

#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `not reporting a reportable death (s 28(1))` EQUALS 10000
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term` `not reporting a reportable death (s 28(1))` EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `not giving the information, or the medical opinion, when notifying a death (s 28(2))` EQUALS 5000
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term` `not giving the information, or the medical opinion, when notifying a death (s 28(2))` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `a false or misleading written statement given in evidence, knowing it to be so (s 20(6))` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term` `a false or misleading written statement given in evidence, knowing it to be so (s 20(6))` EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `hindering or obstructing a person exercising a power or executing a warrant under s 22, or not complying with their direction, other than the Court's (s 22(5)(d))` EQUALS 10000
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term` `hindering or obstructing a person exercising a power or executing a warrant under s 22, or not complying with their direction, other than the Court's (s 22(5)(d))` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `divulging information obtained in the administration of the Act (s 34)` EQUALS 10000
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term` `divulging information obtained in the administration of the Act (s 34)` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `a contempt of the Coroner's Court (ss 22(5)(c), 23(4), 36)` EQUALS 10000
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term` `a contempt of the Coroner's Court (ss 22(5)(c), 23(4), 36)` EQUALS 2

§§ `ss 22, 29, 32: the body and the cause of death`

`not yet held` MEANS `not yet: the body is under the exclusive control of the State Coroner until the State Coroner considers it is not further required for an inquest and issues an authorisation for its disposal (s 32(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released to us` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not yet held`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released to us` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the State Coroner has issued an authorisation for the disposal of human remains, so the body is no longer under the State Coroner's exclusive control (s 32(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released to us` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not yet: the State Coroner knows of a dispute about who is entitled to the body for its disposal, and may hold back the authorisation until the dispute is resolved (s 32(2))`
-- Once the authorisation issues, a dispute no longer holds it back under s 32.
#ASSERT `Can the body be released to us` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the State Coroner has issued an authorisation for the disposal of human remains, so the body is no longer under the State Coroner's exclusive control (s 32(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released to us` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 32 does not hold the body: it applies only to a reportable death where the body is in South Australia (s 32(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released to us` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 32 does not hold the body: it applies only to a reportable death where the body is in South Australia (s 32(1))`

#ASSERT `Can the coroner do this to the body` `a post-mortem examination` `the State Coroner, deciding whether to hold an inquest` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a pathologist or other suitably qualified person may be directed to perform a post-mortem examination and any further examinations or tests; the Act asks for no consent from the family (s 22(1)(i))`
#ASSERT `Can the coroner do this to the body` `removing the body to a specified place` `the Coroner's Court, for an inquest` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a warrant may be issued to remove the body to a specified place (s 22(1)(g))`
#ASSERT `Can the coroner do this to the body` `exhuming the body, or retrieving the ashes` `the Coroner's Court, for an inquest` FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Court may issue an exhumation warrant (s 22(1)(h))`
#ASSERT `Can the coroner do this to the body` `exhuming the body, or retrieving the ashes` `the State Coroner, deciding whether to hold an inquest` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the State Coroner may issue an exhumation warrant with the Attorney-General's approval (s 22(1)(h), (2))`
#ASSERT `Can the coroner do this to the body` `exhuming the body, or retrieving the ashes` `the State Coroner, deciding whether to hold an inquest` FALSE EQUALS `no: an exhumation warrant of the State Coroner may only be issued with the Attorney-General's approval (s 22(2))`

#ASSERT `Who finds the cause of death` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 29 does not apply: it applies only to a reportable death notified to the State Coroner (s 29(1))`
#ASSERT `Who finds the cause of death` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the Coroner's Court will make the finding as to the cause of death, because an inquest is held or required (s 29(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Who finds the cause of death` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the State Coroner may find that the death was due to undetermined natural causes, without further investigation, inquiry or inquest (s 29(2))`
#ASSERT `Who finds the cause of death` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the State Coroner must make a finding as to the cause of death; a finding of undetermined natural causes needs the State Coroner to have reasonable grounds, after medical information or advice, to believe the death was due to natural causes (s 29(1)(b), (2)(b))`
#ASSERT `Who finds the cause of death` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the State Coroner must make a finding as to the cause of death; a finding of undetermined natural causes needs the written consent of a senior next of kin to no further investigation (s 29(1)(b), (2)(c))`

§§ `ss 34, 37, 37A: records and information`

`by permission` MEANS `only with the State Coroner's permission, which may be given on conditions (including limits on publication or use); the decision is final and not subject to review (s 37(2)-(4))`
`as of right` MEANS `yes: on your application the State Coroner must let you inspect it or obtain a copy; a fee fixed by regulation may be charged (s 37(1), (5))`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `a transcript of evidence` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `as of right`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `process forming part of the Court's records` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `as of right`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `documentary material admitted into evidence` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `as of right`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `a transcript of the written findings and recommendations` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `as of right`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `an order of the Court` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `as of right`
-- s 37(2) overrides s 37(1) ("Subject to this section"): each of (a) to (d) alone.
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `documentary material admitted into evidence` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `by permission`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `documentary material admitted into evidence` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `by permission`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `documentary material admitted into evidence` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `by permission`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `documentary material admitted into evidence` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `by permission`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `other material` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `by permission`
#ASSERT `Can I see the inquest material` `other material` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 37 gives access only to process, transcripts of evidence, documentary exhibits, findings and recommendations, and orders`

-- s 37A(2): finding 10 May 2000; the 20 years end on 10 May 2020; the first day to apply is 11 May 2020.
#ASSERT `From when can our family apply for the records` (`cor date` 10 5 2000) EQUALS `cor date` 11 5 2020
-- 29 February 2004 -> 29 February 2024 (leap) -> 1 March 2024; 29 February 2080 -> 28 February 2100 (not leap) -> 1 March 2100.
#ASSERT `From when can our family apply for the records` (`cor date` 29 2 2004) EQUALS `cor date` 1 3 2024
#ASSERT `From when can our family apply for the records` (`cor date` 29 2 2080) EQUALS `cor date` 1 3 2100

`records yes` MEANS `yes: the State Coroner must give you, free of charge, a copy of all records held about the event; it may impose conditions on their use, redact identities where the interests of justice require, and release suppressed material only if satisfied that justice requires it (s 37A(1), (5)-(8))`
`records not yet` MEANS `not yet: an application may be made only after 20 years from the finding (for a reportable death) or from the decision not to hold an inquest (s 37A(2))`
#ASSERT `Must the State Coroner give us the records` TRUE TRUE (`cor date` 10 5 2000) (`cor date` 11 5 2020) FALSE EQUALS `records yes`
#ASSERT `Must the State Coroner give us the records` TRUE TRUE (`cor date` 10 5 2000) (`cor date` 10 5 2020) FALSE EQUALS `records not yet`
#ASSERT `Must the State Coroner give us the records` TRUE TRUE (`cor date` 10 5 2000) (`cor date` 11 5 2020) TRUE EQUALS `no: the State Coroner is satisfied that providing the records is not in the interests of justice (s 37A(1))`
#ASSERT `Must the State Coroner give us the records` TRUE FALSE (`cor date` 10 5 2000) (`cor date` 11 5 2020) FALSE EQUALS `no: only the family of the person may apply under s 37A (s 37A(1), (9))`
#ASSERT `Must the State Coroner give us the records` FALSE TRUE (`cor date` 10 5 2000) (`cor date` 11 5 2020) FALSE EQUALS `s 37A does not apply: the State Coroner has not determined that no inquest is to be held into the event; s 37 governs access to inquest material (s 37A(1), (3))`

#ASSERT `May I pass on the information` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the information is publicly known (s 34(a))`
#ASSERT `May I pass on the information` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: this Act or another Act or law requires or authorises it (s 34(b))`
#ASSERT `May I pass on the information` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is reasonably required in connection with the administration of this Act or another Act (s 34(c))`
#ASSERT `May I pass on the information` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is for legal proceedings arising out of the administration of this Act (s 34(d))`
#ASSERT `May I pass on the information` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is to a government agency or instrumentality for the proper performance of its functions (s 34(e))`
#ASSERT `May I pass on the information` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the person to whom the information relates consents (s 34(f))`
#ASSERT `May I pass on the information` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: none of the exceptions in s 34 applies; divulging it is an offence (maximum penalty $10 000)`

§§ `ss 33, 35: claims against the system`

#ASSERT `Can I hold them liable` `a coroner` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a coroner has the same protections, privileges and immunities from liability as a Judge of the Supreme Court (s 33(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I hold them liable` `a member of the Court's staff, an investigator, or a person assisting an investigator` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: they incur no civil or criminal liability for an honest act or omission in carrying out, or purportedly carrying out, official functions (s 33(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I hold them liable` `a member of the Court's staff, an investigator, or a person assisting an investigator` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 33(2) gives them no immunity: the act or omission was not honest, or not in carrying out official functions`
#ASSERT `Can I hold them liable` `a member of the Court's staff, an investigator, or a person assisting an investigator` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 33(2) gives them no immunity: the act or omission was not honest, or not in carrying out official functions`

#ASSERT `Can I call the coroner as a witness` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: a coroner cannot be called to give evidence before a court or tribunal about anything that came to the coroner's knowledge in the administration of the Act (s 35(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I call the coroner as a witness` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 35 does not stop it: the proceedings are against the coroner for an offence (s 35(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I call the coroner as a witness` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 35 does not stop it: the evidence is not about something that came to the coroner's knowledge in the administration of the Act`
